## Design questions (answer first, with evidence)

Q1. Repo shape
- Is this a monorepo, a polyrepo (this folder is one service), or a mix?
- List top-level service/app/package directories.
- Evidence: paths (e.g. services/, apps/, cmd/, docker-compose.yml).

Q2. How is a "service" defined here?
- One deployable? One repo? One Helm chart? One package.json?
- Name the rule you will use, then list service ids (stable kebab-case).

Q3. Languages and frameworks
- For each service: language, framework (Spring, Nest, FastAPI, Go chi, etc.).
- Evidence: package.json, go.mod, pom.xml, pyproject.toml, *.csproj.

Q4. How do services call each other?
For each mechanism found, give examples with file paths:
- HTTP/REST (base URLs, service names in env, OpenAPI/Swagger)
- gRPC (.proto, generated clients)
- GraphQL
- Message buses (Kafka/SQS/SNS/Rabbit/NATS topic names)
- DB/cache (Postgres, Redis) — these are infra nodes
- Mesh/ingress (Istio, NGINX, API gateway, BFF)

Q5. Entry points
- Web apps, mobile backends, API gateways, cron/workers.
- What nothing else calls (likely edge).

Q6. Infra / shared platforms
- Auth, identity, databases, queues, object storage, third-party APIs.
- Treat these as nodes if multiple services depend on them.

Q7. Ownership and docs
- CODEOWNERS, CODEOWNERS-like files, catalog (catalog-info.yaml, service.yaml), README architecture sections.
- For each service: owner team or "unknown".
- List architecture docs/wikis in-repo that claim dependencies.

Q8. Resilience in code
- Circuit breakers, retries, timeouts, bulkheads (Resilience4j, Polly, opossum, Istio DestinationRule, etc.).
- File paths. Which edge they protect.

Q9. Cycles
- Any A→B→C→A in declared calls? List them. Do not force a cycle.

Q10. What NOT to include
- Dev-only tools, one-off scripts, generated node_modules, vendored code, test fakes unless they are real prod services.

